WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:07.280
I had the idea to do a 10k Q&amp;A sort of thing&nbsp;
about a month ago when I was at 9000 subscribers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:13.320
and somehow the passage of time took&nbsp;
place and now I’m creeping up on 15k,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:20.160
so I don’t really know how that happened. But in&nbsp;
all seriousness, I appreciate the support, and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:26.000
I dunno, It’s cool to make things and have a lot&nbsp;
of people enjoy those things, so that’s been cool.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.640
Thank you! Part of the reason I’m making this is&nbsp;
that I want to share some updates about what’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:37.640
going on with the channel and what I want to do&nbsp;
with it. You may have noticed that I”m posting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:43.440
more recently, and indeed, I’m posting weekly&nbsp;
now, on thursdays. ANd I’m currently trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:50.880
alternate between the higher effort content, the&nbsp;
deep dives into a particular deckbuilding subject,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:57.560
and the more fun content, where I, for example,&nbsp;
analyze a precon, or just talk about whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:03.680
it is I want to talk about that day. So&nbsp;
that’s currently what I’m trying to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:11.720
and one particular type of off-week content&nbsp;
that I’m going to start next month is Patron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:18.960
deck reviews. These deck reviews are going to&nbsp;
be very much in the style of the precon review,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:25.520
so I’m going to try and figure out what a deck is&nbsp;
trying to do, how well it’s doing at that goal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:30.560
and some potential routes for improvement. So, as&nbsp;
I said, that’s going to be starting up next month.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.520
I’m going to be picking decks through a lottery&nbsp;
system, and you can check out my Patreon for more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:42.360
information about that. Also on my patreon, I’m&nbsp;
going to be posting a Glissa deck tech very soon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:49.040
so that’s two different reasons to check out my&nbsp;
Patreon, that’s pretty cool. With that out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:56.920
way, let’s get to questions, and these are mostly&nbsp;
taken from a post I made on Patreon recently:

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:06.080
About a year ago I needed a username for some&nbsp;
website or other, and my two second on-the-spot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:12.480
smash together of words was “Salubrious Snail”. A&nbsp;
few months later, I was trying to come up with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:18.640
name for my youtube channel, and that one popped&nbsp;
back into my brain, and I kind of liked it. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:26.200
snappy, it’s alliterative, it has a fun word,&nbsp;
seems pretty cool. So that’s what I went with.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:38.720
I learned to play magic in 2011, and the way I got&nbsp;
into the game is that Mark Rosewater gave a career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:45.080
day presentation at my middle school, and at the&nbsp;
end of it he handed all these little 11 and 12&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:56.200
year olds starter decks from M12 (I got the red&nbsp;
one) and then I just went from there. I started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:04.200
playing limited in 2013, so my first draft was&nbsp;
Dragon’s Maze, and then in early 2014 I really got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:13.080
into the game; I started playing standard and EDH.&nbsp;
I’ve played kind of off and on from them, picking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:20.600
back up very heavily in early last year, starting&nbsp;
to build a whole bunch more commander decks.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:31.560
I’ve always been a black player, and my first&nbsp;
deck that I can remember was a sort of black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:38.000
lifegain-ish deck. It ran a lot of removal&nbsp;
because that’s just who I am as a player,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:44.960
and the specific interaction I can remember&nbsp;
between two cards was that it had Tormented Soul,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:53.200
an unblockable creature, and Vampire Outcasts, a&nbsp;
bloodthirst creature, one that enters larger if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:58.440
an opponent was dealt damage this turn, so it&nbsp;
almost always enters as a 4 mana 4/4 lifelink,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:04.040
which, honestly, not bad. Lifelink is&nbsp;
one of the best keywords. Pretty solid.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:15.280
That one! Ashiok, Nightmare Weaver has always been&nbsp;
a card I love, and it’s funny because I feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:22.000
it’s known for cube, y’know, you do the Splashiok,&nbsp;
but I’ve never played with it in cube. The place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:29.160
I’ve played with it was in standard Grixis&nbsp;
control. I ran 3 of it, and it was fantastic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:35.480
In the faster matchups, it soaked up damage&nbsp;
for my deck, which sadly did not run lifegain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:40.400
and in the control matchups, of which there&nbsp;
were many because that was standard at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:47.840
all the abilities were great. The +2, you can&nbsp;
exile cards, which was great against Azorious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:54.080
control which cycled through its deck repeatedly.&nbsp;
The -10, if your opponent lets you get up there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:59.720
just blows out a control deck, so just a&nbsp;
great card all around. I also love the flavor;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:06.280
I’m a big horror fan, and the idea of this&nbsp;
unknowable, otherworldly entity that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:11.080
weaponizing the fears of its foes to destroy&nbsp;
them, I love that. That’s incredible to me.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:21.480
This is a good question, and I think for&nbsp;
starters it’s good to make sure that decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:28.200
are at similar at power levels. Figure out what&nbsp;
decks are doing, how fast they’re doing them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:34.560
and just make sure things are compatible and there&nbsp;
aren’t going to be crazy discrepancies. Secondly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:41.840
for middling to lower power levels, it’s good to&nbsp;
check in with what people’s intentions are for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:49.840
game and what they find fun. There are things&nbsp;
like board wipes or combos or heavy duty stax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:54.320
pieces where people might not have as much fun if&nbsp;
they’re playing against these things; that might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.320 --> 00:06:02.160
not be a thing they want to see a lot of in their&nbsp;
game, and I think it’s fair to navigate that to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:09.320
some extent. I will sometimes ask somebody not&nbsp;
to play a particular deck, even. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:14.440
I have a friend who has a Tergrid deck, and three&nbsp;
nights out of four I don’t want to play against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:23.720
it. And I think that’s fair, although there&nbsp;
are some limits to what you can request of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:30.520
somebody and it’s a tricky thing to navigate, you&nbsp;
can’t just ask that every deck your deck is bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:39.080
against isn’t in the game, that’s not really&nbsp;
fair. At the same time, when I build a deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:46.400
if it’s a particularly feisty or mean or&nbsp;
suppressive deck I have some understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:56.600
that it might see less action than a deck&nbsp;
that is nicer or offends people a little less.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:07.400
So, the edh ban list is tricky because it’s&nbsp;
really trying to be two different things:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:14.200
it’s trying to dictate what’s too strong for cEDH&nbsp;
and it’s also trying to dictate what’s not fun for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:19.360
casual, and I think answering these two questions&nbsp;
at the same time is difficult. We can think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:27.200
some different cards we might think of banning.&nbsp;
Dockside Extortionist, for example—good card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:33.480
maybe it should be banned. But that would&nbsp;
mainly be a ban for cEDH and the top levels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:41.280
of high power casual, because it doesn’t matter&nbsp;
for random midrange beats decks, there aren’t that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:46.240
many artifacts at that power level, you aren’t&nbsp;
going to be doing the same sort of value loops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:54.920
it just won’t have the same horrifying power&nbsp;
level as in other EDH contexts. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.920 --> 00:08:02.960
we can think about Armageddon. We could ban&nbsp;
it because it’s not fun, but that’s not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:09.160
ban in the conventional sense of a card game,&nbsp;
you’re not banning it because it’s too strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:14.200
you’re banning it because it’s unfun. And I think&nbsp;
there is a little bit of tension there in trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:26.600
to solve two different problems. I wouldn’t be&nbsp;
that comfortable dictating a bunch of new card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:31.760
allowances and disallowances for EDH because&nbsp;
I think it’s a really hard thing to do. What I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:40.120
would encourage is players having more of a rule 0&nbsp;
conversation, a conversation about what they find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:47.960
fun and what they don’t want to see in their game.&nbsp;
You shouldn’t roll up into a game with strangers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:53.200
and blow up all their lands if they’re just trying&nbsp;
to play a game with upgraded precons or something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:58.080
I think it’s good to have an understanding about&nbsp;
that, and I think this is an acceptable solution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:10.200
since EDH is a social format fundamentally.&nbsp;
If I had to ban one card for very petty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:17.600
personal reasons, it would be Farewell. I hate&nbsp;
the card. I think it’s very lazy design. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:23.440
a 6 mana board wipe that has no downsides other&nbsp;
than that it’s a 6 mana board wipe; it’s probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:29.680
not broken from a power level stand point,&nbsp;
but I just hate to see it. It’s lazy. It also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:41.280
crowds out other cards like Merciless Eviction or&nbsp;
Austere Command. So yeah, not a fan of Farewell.

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:53.560
The cat came back. I actually really like&nbsp;
explicitly political cards. Council’s Dilemma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.560 --> 00:10:00.320
and Will of the Council are both cool mechanics&nbsp;
with cool design space. It’s unfortunate that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:07.440
two cards from those that actually ended up being&nbsp;
good in EDH are some of the less interesting ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:10.160
but I think there’s plenty of other design&nbsp;
space, and I think it’d be cool if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:17.360
printed more of those cards. I also think&nbsp;
goad is a super cool mechanic, love goad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:24.600
and I think it’d be cool to expand the mindset of&nbsp;
goad outside of creatures because I think goad has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:35.160
a natural power limitation, that it’s not going&nbsp;
to be good above the beaty midrange power level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:38.200
and I think that’s a valid thing for a&nbsp;
card to be, but I think it’d be cool to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:46.040
see more explicitly political cards that play&nbsp;
with other resources such as cards in hand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:54.440
Demonstrate, the mechanic where you can copy&nbsp;
a spell if you let your opponent have a copy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.960 --> 00:11:01.600
I think that one is kind of cool, so yeah,&nbsp;
I think more stuff like that would be neat.

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:14.320
Sometime last year, I played against a&nbsp;
Tymna Jeska tribal ball lightning deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:24.800
and that deck’s vision is splendid to me,&nbsp;
I love decks that take a really juicy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:33.480
powerful commander or pair of partner commanders&nbsp;
and use them for ridiculous ends. I just love that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:38.800
and I love the idea of swinging with a ball&nbsp;
lightning for 18 at somebody’s face, that’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:47.480
vision I can appreciate. I have to go with that&nbsp;
deck, that’s the only one that comes to mind.

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:56.320
I’ve gotten a handful of comments asking about the&nbsp;
cat post and the implied cat that goes with it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:02.080
and thankfully he made an appearance&nbsp;
